00:00:08.910 --> 00:00:14.469
The Arctic will be perhaps the single most
influential region on earth in the coming

00:00:14.469 --> 00:00:17.840
century and yet almost no-one even lives there.

00:00:17.840 --> 00:00:22.430
Eight nations have territory above the Arctic
Circle—Denmark though their constituent

00:00:22.430 --> 00:00:28.930
country: Greenland, Iceland, Norway, Sweden,
Finland, Russia, the United States, and Canada—and

00:00:28.930 --> 00:00:31.720
they are all in close quarters.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:35.820
This circle represents the distance a plane
can fly in three hours.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:40.129
Most of these countries can reach each other
faster than they watch Titanic.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:45.729
Anchorage, Alaska is, in fact, closer to Tromsø,
Norway than it is to New York because of the

00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:47.350
short-cut over the pole.

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:52.899
Alert, Canada is so close to Tromsø, Norway
that it could be flown by a turboprop plane

00:00:52.899 --> 00:00:53.899
(Pilatus PC-12 NG.)

00:00:53.899 --> 00:00:58.109
The only issue is, there’s about to be some
serious money in the high north.

00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:01.440
The Arctic ice is melting, there’s no question
about that.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:05.259
Some may debate the cause of the melt, but
one cannot debate that there’s simply less

00:01:05.259 --> 00:01:07.880
ice up north than there was 50 years ago.

00:01:07.880 --> 00:01:10.590
This melt has profound consequences.

00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:15.000
Whole countries like Tuvalu and the Maldives
could be largely underwater by the end of

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.799
the century because of the rising sea levels
from melting ice.

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:22.850
But the melt has a different, more obvious
effect—where there was once ice there’s

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:25.020
now liquid, navigable water.

00:01:25.020 --> 00:01:29.770
One of the greatest quests for early explorers
was to find a Northwest Passage—a navigable

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:34.070
sea route connecting the Atlantic and Pacific
Oceans through the Canadian Archipelago.

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:40.579
It was long thought to be myth until in 1906
Roald Amundsen and his six crew members arrived

00:01:40.579 --> 00:01:45.829
at Herchel Island, Canada, having successfully
completed a three-year voyage from Norway

00:01:45.829 --> 00:01:48.810
via the new Northwest Passage.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:53.909
The significance of the Northwest Passage
is that, until 1914 when the Panama Canal

00:01:53.909 --> 00:01:59.380
opened, traffic from the Atlantic could only
reach the Pacific by sailing around Cape Horn—the

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.109
southern tip of South America.

00:02:01.109 --> 00:02:05.450
This meant that a sea route between London
and San Francisco—5,000 miles apart as the

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:08.660
crow flies—took 14,000 miles.

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:10.090
This was not efficient.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:14.290
It was a significant hamper to development
to the American west coast.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:20.140
The Northwest passage would’ve revolutionized
maritime trade—if it wasn't covered in ice.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.950
Roald Amundsen’s ship, the Gjøa, was small
enough that it could snake through and slide

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:26.100
over ice.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.209
Some of the waterways Amundsen took were as
few as three feet deep—far too shallow for

00:02:31.209 --> 00:02:34.300
the increasingly large commercial ships of
the time.

00:02:34.300 --> 00:02:39.769
More than 100 years later, in September of
2013, however, for the very first time, a

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:46.281
commercial bulk carrier, the MS Nordic Orion,
transited an almost ice-free Northwest Passage

00:02:46.281 --> 00:02:51.750
on its journey from Vancouver, Canada to Pori,
Finland, and this was far from a publicity

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:52.750
stunt.

00:02:52.750 --> 00:03:00.129
This ship saved $80,000 in fuel costs and
was able to take 25% more cargo than if it

00:03:00.129 --> 00:03:02.390
had gone through the Panama Canal.

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:05.629
Even thousand passenger cruise ships are now
making the journey.

00:03:05.629 --> 00:03:11.110
Ironically, global warming is actually opening
a route that’s better for the environment.

00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:15.870
China is a country with a vested interest
in the navigability of the northwest passage.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:20.799
As an economy largely based on manufacturing
for the western world, their maritime accessibility

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:23.640
has an enormous effect on their national wellbeing.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:28.269
A reduction in time and cost of shipping to
the American east coast would renew their

00:03:28.269 --> 00:03:33.280
competitiveness in the manufacturing industry
against emerging rivals such as Vietnam and

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:34.280
Bangladesh.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:38.430
As China industrialized largely thanks to
its manufacturing industry the standard of

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:43.269
living in the country increased which correspondently
increased labour costs.

00:03:43.269 --> 00:03:48.040
China’s Maritime Safety Administration,
recognizing the imminent explosion in usage,

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:54.370
recently published a 356 page guide to navigating
the northwest passage and the country has

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:58.830
announced plans to send more and more commercial
shipping traffic through the passage in the

00:03:58.830 --> 00:03:59.920
coming summers.

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:04.120
The introduction of maritime traffic to the
northwest passage could present a significant

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:05.780
opportunity for Canada.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:10.080
The northern territories of Canada, through
which northwest passage runs, are historically

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:11.700
underdeveloped.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.680
Less than 120,000 people live in the Yukon,
the Northwest territories, and Nunavut.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.690
That’s less than the population of Saguenay—a
town small enough that you probably haven’t

00:04:23.690 --> 00:04:27.910
even heard of it—living in an area larger
than the entire country of India.

00:04:27.910 --> 00:04:32.670
It’s not all that surprising considering
just how inhospitable the area is, but other

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:38.130
places at similar latitudes such as Anchorage,
Longyearbyen, and Murmansk have managed to

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:42.430
overcome the conditions thanks to the money
that can be made in the far north.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:46.860
If a large chunk of the worlds maritime traffic
heads through the Canadian north, industry

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.860
will develop to support these ships.

00:04:49.860 --> 00:04:52.370
Except, there’s a problem.

00:04:52.370 --> 00:04:56.850
Despite the general friendliness of most of
the arctic countries, there are geopolitical

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:58.790
issues in the high north.

00:04:58.790 --> 00:05:03.130
Even more surprisingly, one of them is between
the US and Canada.

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.470
When there’s a navigation choke-point restricting
certain countries from accessing an ocean,

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:11.600
it’s convention to declare that waterway
an international waterway.

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.150
For example, the Danish Straits—fully surrounded
by Denmark—are an international waterway

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.820
in order to give the Baltic and Scandinavian
countries ocean access; the Turkish straits,

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.670
fully surrounded by Turkey, are international
waterways to give the black sea countries

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:30.600
ocean access; and the Danube River is an international
waterway to give landlocked Austria, Hungary,

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.800
Moldova, Serbia, and Slovakia ocean access.

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:38.810
When a waterway is declared an international
waterway no country can restrict access or

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:42.910
charge dues to passing boats except during
a time of war.

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:47.130
Canada considers the waterways comprising
the northwest passage in their archipelago

00:05:47.130 --> 00:05:48.870
as their own waters.

00:05:48.870 --> 00:05:52.780
In the past nobody challenged this since there
was no reason anyone would cross through these

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:54.120
frozen waters.

00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:58.270
With its promise to cut shipping routes by
thousands of miles, the northwest passage

00:05:58.270 --> 00:06:02.400
will almost certainly become an important
shipping route so that’s why countries like

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:07.850
the US firmly believe that the northwest passage
should be and already is an international

00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:08.850
waterway.

00:06:08.850 --> 00:06:14.890
One of the tensest moments in history between
the US and Canada was when, in 1985, a US

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.930
Coast Guard Icebreaker travelled through the
northwest passage without prior permission

00:06:18.930 --> 00:06:19.930
from Canada.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:25.581
In Canada’s mind, this was a military invasion
of their sovereign territory—debatably an

00:06:25.581 --> 00:06:26.910
act of war.

00:06:26.910 --> 00:06:31.410
Canada argues that the northwest passage is
not an international waterway because it has

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:35.020
failed to meet an important criteria—usefulness.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:39.180
Of course the northwest passage is useful
on paper—it shortens the route between the

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.460
oceans—but Canada has pointed out that in
previous cases determining whether a waterway

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:48.530
is international, what proves a route’s
usefulness is if a significant number of ships

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:51.040
have already successfully transited it.

00:06:51.040 --> 00:06:54.970
In the northwest passage’s case, the number
of successful commercial journeys is in the

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:55.970
double digits.

00:06:55.970 --> 00:06:59.920
There’s also merit to Canada’s argument
that the passage should be their sovereign

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:00.920
waters.

00:07:00.920 --> 00:07:05.600
Currently, Canada has almost no search-and-rescue
capabilities in their archipelago.

00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:09.680
Since there’s almost no traffic yet, there’s
no real reason to spend the money to put ships

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:11.250
and aircraft up there.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:16.530
Most previous journeys have been highly coordinated
and often escorted by the Canadian Coast Guard.

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:21.020
If a ship just went through with no prior
coordination nowadays and sank, however, there

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.040
would be almost no chance of rescue for the
victims.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:29.390
If in a few summers hundreds of ships transit
the passage, Canada would have an obligation

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:34.800
to put resources in the northern provinces
for the safety of both the country and sailors

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:36.990
and that takes money.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:41.550
If treated as an internal waterway, Canada
could charge passage fees just as there are

00:07:41.550 --> 00:07:46.190
for the Panama or Suez Canal—the other major
shipping shortcuts of the world.

00:07:46.190 --> 00:07:50.750
These could fund the infrastructure needed
to safety regulate and police the route.

00:07:50.750 --> 00:07:54.560
But on the other hand, should one country
have the capability to chose who can get from

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:56.220
the Pacific to Atlantic faster?

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:00.400
Letting, for example, Vietnamese ships through
the route but banning Chinese ships would

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:05.950
make the Chinese goods uncompetitive for the
Western European and Eastern American market.

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:10.350
Canada would have the capability to choose
which economies can succeed and which will

00:08:10.350 --> 00:08:11.350
fail.

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.760
There’s a reason the issue’s so contentious.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.180
Scientists disagree on the exact date, but
there’s a general consensus that by the

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.670
year 2050 there will be a summer when there
is no ice in the Arctic.

00:08:22.670 --> 00:08:28.660
This will have enormous and irreversible consequences
on our globe, but it could further revolutionize

00:08:28.660 --> 00:08:29.890
how we get our goods.

00:08:29.890 --> 00:08:35.759
An ice-free arctic will open up the greatest
shipping short-cut in the world—the Arctic

00:08:35.759 --> 00:08:36.759
Ocean.

00:08:36.759 --> 00:08:40.899
Ships traveling between Japan and western
Europe, for example, instead of heading south,

00:08:40.899 --> 00:08:45.060
across the Indian Ocean, through the Suez
Canal, and across the Mediterranean sea, will

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:49.959
be able to head north through the bering strait,
directly across the arctic ocean, and down

00:08:49.959 --> 00:08:52.170
between Greenland and Norway to Europe.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:57.350
That’s a 7,000 mile route compared to the
13,000 mile route of today.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:00.459
That has the potential to slash shipping prices
in half.

00:09:00.459 --> 00:09:03.629
That means cheaper products across the entire
world.

00:09:03.629 --> 00:09:05.430
But at what cost.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:11.629
Every degree of climate warming in the US
alone is expected to cause $144 billion dollars

00:09:11.629 --> 00:09:13.620
per year of economic loss.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:19.970
If the US climate warms by 12 degrees, which
the EPA says is possible by 2100, the US can

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:25.579
expect to lose more than $1.7 trillion per
year—that’s more than a full percent of

00:09:25.579 --> 00:09:26.680
its GDP.

00:09:26.680 --> 00:09:33.240
On top of that, by 2050, climate change is
expected to cause more than 250,000 deaths

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:34.240
per year.

00:09:34.240 --> 00:09:39.420
Surely that can’t be worth it for some cheaper
goods.

